{
  "description": "Shared TS<->Python cross-runtime projection parity fixture (AUDIT-02). Both the Python builder test (workers/automation/tests/test_audit_projection_parity.py) and the TS builder test (apps/api/test/audit-projection-parity.test.ts) seed the SAME canonical rows, run their OWN projection builder, and assert the resulting projection columns match. `rows` are the canonical SQL rows exactly as the Python repositories write them. `expected` is the audit read shape both builders own (employer analysis verbatim; provenance/coverage/voice converted to camelCase DTOs by the read model). `expectedProjections` is the FULL dual-written column set for job_list_projections / job_detail_projections / dashboard_projections: every deterministic column each builder writes, with *_json columns given as parsed objects (both runtimes serialise with different whitespace/key order, so the tests parse before comparing). `projectionParity.jsonColumns` marks which columns to parse; `projectionParity.nonDeterministicColumns` marks wall-clock columns (last_updated_at / generated_at) that are asserted present-and-non-null but excluded from value comparison. Each test also runs a column-set guard: the set of columns its builder emits must equal the expectedProjections keys plus the non-deterministic columns, so a one-sided column addition in EITHER builder's schema fails the shared fixture. This closes the gap that let the Python-omits-score-audit-columns drift ship: the earlier fixture only asserted 4 audit JSON columns and no column-set guard. `dashboardAggregateJobs` seeds extra tenant-level jobs that feed ONLY the dashboard aggregate (the job_list/job_detail assertions target the primary `job` only): a non-hidden apply/pending job with no resume (so dashboard.ready genuinely asserts the has_resume predicate) and a hidden applied job in jobhunter_hidden_jobs (so total_jobs/applied/by_source/score_distribution/funnel genuinely assert hidden-job exclusion). The primary job.location is a non-normalized value (\"Remote - Berlin, Germany\") whose expected job_list.location is the normalized form (\"Berlin, Germany (Remote)\"), so both builders must run the same location normalization. These three columns (location, ready, hidden-job totals) were previously worked around; both builders now converge on them (B2b). `rows.conversionJobs` seed two extra applied+scored greenhouse jobs and `rows.applicationOutcomes` record their outcomes (plus the primary job's) so `dashboard.outcome_conversion_json` is a non-trivial funnel with multi-entry bySource/byBand; the hidden applied job carries no outcome and, being hidden, is excluded from the funnel denominator, so the conversion totals also prove hidden-job exclusion flows through the rollup (#223). With the primary job, the no-resume job, and the two conversion jobs all active (the hidden job excluded) the dashboard aggregates over four jobs: total_jobs 4, applied 3, ready 0, funnel 4/4 per stage except apply (3 succeeded, 1 pending), by_source greenhouse 3 / linkedin 1, score_distribution 8/7/6. The top-level `locationCases` array is a shared TS<->Python table of raw->normalized location inputs consumed by BOTH apps/api/test/location-normalization.test.ts and workers/automation/tests/test_location_normalization.py (which replaced their previously hand-maintained, already-drifted case lists), so neither normalization implementation can change a Spain region label (AN/CT/MD), a country token (ES/ESP/Spain/Espana), or a remote token/marker (remote, en remoto, remoto, teletrabajo, work from home, wfh) without one of those tests going red. It exercises the region-label expansion branch (gated on a Spain country token being present) and the remote-marker branch (every remote token plus the parenthetical markers).",
